orting), SD has the highest average rating\n","   (4.00 stars) and PR has the lowest\n","   (2.20 stars).\n","\n","4. Hospitals with under 100 completed surveys average 4.67 stars, compared to\n","   3.20 stars for hospitals with over 1,000 completed surveys.\n","   This gap is worth flagging as a possible small-sample bias.\n","\n","5. About 0.2% of hospitals show a real gap (2+ stars) between \"overall rating\"\n","   and \"would recommend\" — meaning these two are usually consistent, but not always.\n","\n"]}],"source":["lowest = national_avg.iloc[0]\n","highest_corr = correlations.iloc[0]\n","lowest_corr = correlations.iloc[-1]\n","best_state = state_summary.iloc[0]\n","worst_state = state_summary.iloc[-1]\n","small_n = q4[q4[\"survey_size_group\"] == \"Under 100\"][\"avg_overall_rating\"].values\n","large_n = q4[q4[\"survey_size_group\"] == \"Over 1000\"][\"avg_overall_rating\"].values\n","\n","print(f'''\n","WHAT I FOUND (HCAHPS hospital patient-experience data, national, one survey year)\n","\n","1. Of the 6 specific care measures, \"{lowest['measure']}\" scores the lowest nationally,\n","   averaging {lowest['avg_star_rating']:.2f} out of 5 stars.\n","\n","2. \"{highest_corr['measure']}\" is the measure most closely linked to a hospital's overall rating\n","   (correlation of {highest_corr['correlation_with_overall_rating']:.2f}), while\n","   \"{lowest_corr['measure']}\" is the least closely linked ({lowest_corr['correlation_with_overall_rating']:.2f}).\n","   That's useful: it tells a hospital exactly where effort is most likely to move the needle on the overall score.\n","\n","3. By state (10+ hospitals reporting), {best_state['state']} has the highest average rating\n","   ({best_state['avg_overall_rating']:.2f} stars) and {worst_state['state']} has the lowest\n","   ({worst_state['avg_overall_rating']:.2f} stars).\n","\n","4. Hospitals with under 100 completed surveys average {small_n[0]:.2f} stars, compared to\n","   {large_n[0]:.2f} stars for hospitals with over 1,000 completed surveys.\n","   {\"This gap is worth flagging as a possible small-sample bias.\" if abs(small_n[0]-large_n[0]) >= 0.2 else \"The gap here is small, so small-sample bias doesn't look like a major issue in this data.\"}\n","\n","5. About {mismatch_pct:.1f}% of hospitals show a real gap (2+ stars) between \"overall rating\"\n","   and \"would recommend\" — meaning these two are usually consistent, but not always.\n","''')"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"814812a2","metadata":{"id":"814812a2"},"source":["## Step 9: Limits of this analysis (honest notes)\n","\n","- **Suppressed ratings aren't random.** CMS hides the star rating for hospitals with too few responses, on purpose, to avoid publishing unreliable numbers. I treated those as missing rather than as zero or average, but it does mean smaller or less-visited hospitals are underrepresented in this analysis.\n","\n","- *About a third of hospitals are missing entirely. 1,607 of the original 4,790 hospitals had every single one of their 9 measures suppressed, so this analysis only reflects the roughly 3,183 hospitals that had at least one usable score.\n","\n","\n","- **Correlation is not causation.** A measure being closely linked to the overall rating doesn't prove that fixing it causes the overall rating to rise, only that they tend to move together.\n","One survey year. This covers October 2024 to September 2025 discharges. I can't say whether any of this is improving or getting worse over time from a single year\n","\n","- **One survey year.** This covers October 2024 to September 2025 discharges. I can't say whether any of this is improving or getting worse over time from a single year\n","\n","\n","- **State averages mix very different hospitals.** AA state's number blends huge city hospitals and small rural ones together, so it's a starting point for investigation, not a verdict on any one hospital.Patients who respond may not represent all patients. Survey response rates are often well under 50%, so people with especially strong (good or bad) experiences may be more likely to respond\n","\n","- **Patients who respond may not represent all patients.** Survey response rates are often well under 50%, so people with especially strong (good or bad) experiences may be more likely to respond.\n","\n","\n","\n","## Recommendations (what I would tell the Chief Experience Officer)\n","\n","1. **Start with the measure most correlated with overall rating**, not the lowest-scoring one. A measure can score low nationally without being what actually drives patient opinion.\n","2. **Don't over-interpret small hospitals' extreme scores.** In this data, a group with only 3 hospitals showed an average more than a full star higher than every other group a result like that reflects too few hospitals to trust, not a real pattern.\n","3. **Use state comparisons to find where to look, not to judge individual hospitals.** A hospital-by-hospital drill-down is the fair next step.\n","4. **Track \"would recommend\" alongside \"overall rating\"**, since the small gap between them can point to specific dissatisfaction that a single overall score would hide.\n","\n","---\n","*Data source: Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS), Patient Survey (HCAHPS) - Hospital, pulled live via the CMS Provider Data API (data.cms.gov).*"]}],"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"display_name":"Python 3","language":"python","name":"python3"},"colab":{"provenance":[]}},"nbformat":4,"nbformat_minor":5}